so during your studies you're probably  really busy trying to manage courses  friends family etc but you also want to  get these opportunities while still  being able to watch your favorite  netflix shows so i compiled a list of  three tips that i wish i knew during my  bachelor's and my master's that allowed  me to get a lot more opportunities later  in life so the first tip i want to give  is to put your work online as soon as  you start university in any type of way  because that's something i really regret  it so austen cleon says in his book show  your work that if your work is not  online it doesn't really exist and i  actually agree with this so i would  encourage you to think about what you  use in your studies or during your  studies and try to see if you can  somehow reinvent it and put it online so  think about if you're doing coding if  you're using some github code think  about publishing your code as well or if  you love watching study with me youtube  tutorials or videos think about making  one yourself or even if you're reading a  lot of interesting articles i think it's  really nice if you put it on twitter for  example and shout out an article that  you really like and i think by doing  this you kind of build your own  knowledge system a little bit faster  because by trying to explain something  that you learned to someone else that is  so much more difficult than just  learning it for yourself and i think  secondly it is also a really good way to  build up your cv so during my bachelor's  i did a lot of computational projects  but i never put them on github and i now  kind of regret that because it would  have been really good during any type of  technical interview to show what i had  already accomplished on github and i  think lastly it can also really create  these kind of golden nuggets so if you  for example write a blog post on your  first year of your bachelor and you do  this every week at the end of the year  you would have 52 blog posts that could  perhaps get picked up by someone that  really likes it and i think by doing  this you can kind of create your own  following so the second advice i would  give to my younger self is to look into  lab rotations internships and  scholarships as soon as possible because  when i was still living in the  netherlands i thought people like me  couldn't really get a scholarship in my  home country but actually if you look at  countries abroad a lot of countries are  offering specifically for foreign  students really good scholarships or  really good lab rotations slash  internships so i think trying to find  internships laborations and scholarships  is actually not as hard as you think if  you're willing to look outside of your  own country and the added benefit of  that is that you get a really good  travel experience so one of the places i  would look if you want to study in any  country in the european union you have  these marie curry brands that you can  apply to and they're relatively not  super difficult to get i think so yeah  and also for lab rotations something i  did during my bachelor is that i just  emailed professors that i really liked  and this way i scored a really cool  laboration in germany which i also got a  small stipend for so i think just trying  to reach out to people and ask if they  have any type of opportunity for you  even if you're just a bachelor or just a  first year i think this is a really good  experience and kind of teaches you how  to apply for scholarships slash  laborations slash internships and also  how to get them and the last thing to  get more opportunities is also to be  open and willing to give more  opportunities to others so i think if  you are a little bit further in your  studies to help for example  undergraduates or some of your  colleagues finish some of their papers  or thesis is a really good way to get  more opportunities yourself as well  because i think training juniors helping  friends with papers and joining bigger  projects in the end you can become for  example a second author or you can have  just a really good learning experience  of mentoring someone because i think  mentoring people is not valued enough  but i do think if you are a really good  mentor usually trainees really  appreciate you for the longer time so i  would really encourage you if you are a  little bit further in your studies to  also spread the knowledge that you have  gathered so thank you for listening to  this video and if you are struggling a  little bit with your productivity i also  made another video specifically about  productivity and otherwise i wish you a  really good week and see you next time  bye
 

